2] Checking if the i-th Bit is Set :

Approach 1 : Left Shift (Masking)

Create a mask having only the i-th bit set.

1 << i

Then perform AND with the given number.

If the result is non-zero, then the i-th bit is set.
Otherwise, it is not set.

Example:

n = 13

Binary:

13 = 1101

Check the 2nd bit

Mask:

1 << 2

0001 -> 0100

Perform AND

1101
0100
----
0100

Result is non-zero.

Hence, the 2nd bit is set.

Approach 2 : Right Shift

Shift the number i positions to the right.

n >> i

Now the required bit becomes the last bit.

Perform AND with 1.

If the result is 1, then the bit is set.
Otherwise, it is not.

Example:

13 = 1101

Shift by 2

1101 >> 2

0011

Now check the last bit

0011
0001
----
0001

Result is 1.

Hence, the 2nd bit is set.

Why AND?

AND returns 1 only when both bits are 1.

So,

n & (1 << i)

checks whether the i-th bit in n is 1 or not.

Time Complexity: O(1)
Only bitwise operations are performed.

Space Complexity: O(1)
No extra space is used.

Code (Left Shift) :

class Solution {
public:
    bool isBitSet(int n, int i) {
        return (n & (1 << i)) != 0;
    }
};

Code (Right Shift) :

class Solution {
public:
    bool isBitSet(int n, int i) {
        return ((n >> i) & 1) != 0;
    }
};
